## Source map and behavior

- native.go uses cumulative arena offsets and rn_verify_v2. It checks the bitmap
  against every status and rejects out-of-batch bits. Source and arena remain
  C-owned and alive through return, including cancellation. Error results are
  zeroed. Evaluate allocation/native-call failure returns execution_failure;
  acknowledged-job failure stops with diagnostics and retains durable pending.
- wire.go accepts high/normal, default normal. Priority is admission metadata,
  excluded from the payload digest. Retries preserve the original priority.
- service.go keeps FIFO normal/high queues. When both contain runnable work,
  dispatch H,H,H,N; with one runnable queue, leave the cycle unchanged. Cancelled
  queued entries are pruned first. Selection and reservation of one of four
  shared native slots happen under the state mutex. Four workers call native
  code outside it. Evaluate shares capacity and wakes workers on completion.
- recovery.go validates schema 1 and preserves pending priority, cancellation,
  receipts and indexes. Rebuild each queue in admission order. V1 normal jobs
  recover at normal priority. Cancelled pending work becomes ready without
  native dispatch. Incompatible/corrupt databases are rejected, never rebuilt.
- main.go, transport.go, reuse/, build.sh and test.sh are unchanged.

The state mutex still spans complete RocksDB writes, so cancellation cannot
finish between the terminal decision and C call_entry. Cancellation intent is
durable before acknowledgement. Running jobs retain payloads through native
return; completed jobs behind the admission head remain cancellable. Terminal
writes atomically add receipts, remove pending/cancel keys, release accounting
and advance the contiguous checkpoint regardless of dispatch priority.

Baseline durability remains one job per admission/terminal WriteBatch, WAL on,
sync=true. Storage settings: compression off, 128 MiB cache, 64 MiB write buffer,
two buffers/background jobs. Native RocksDB LOCK enforces exclusive ownership.
The 1,024-job/64-MiB limits include completed-but-uncommitted work.

Admission responses precede terminal events, which follow durable commitment.
Events remain best-effort; status recovers notification loss. Disconnect does not
cancel. Drain waits for accepted jobs, evaluations and active replies. Identical
retries remain existing during draining and never alter priority or ownership.

## Validation and evidence

The first full ./test.sh passed all 17 Go test families, Go race tests, public
smoke, ordinary socket/crash integration and race executable integration.
No maintenance build/test failed and no repair round was used.

New tests cover priority parsing/retries, FIFO/3:1 selection, single-queue and
idle cycle preservation, cancelled entries, slot saturation, mixed v1/v2
recovery, corrupt priority rejection and 1/63/64-item bitmap/offset boundaries.
Existing admission limits, cancellation, wire framing, native results and
shutdown/reply checks remain. The four-slot ownership test arranges in-flight
jobs through the real dispatch function without production barriers or hooks.

Final integration artifacts:

- tmp/v2-e2e-0yk39pup: ordinary executable; 193 acknowledged cancellations,
  three mixed-priority v2 crashes and one actual v1-to-v2 upgrade.
- tmp/v2-e2e-0smr90u5: race executable; 153 acknowledged cancellations,
  three mixed-priority v2 crashes and one actual v1-to-v2 upgrade, recovering
  eight pending v1 jobs.

Physical database checks run only after the writer stops. All eight crash runs
preserved acknowledged admissions/cancellations, existing receipts and accounting,
and drained after recovery. The ordinary upgrade crash had no pending jobs;
the race upgrade and deterministic fixtures supply pending-v1 recovery evidence.
The executable upgrade test runs when tmp/predecessor/service exists; the Go
compatibility fixture always runs. See EXPERIMENTS.md for per-run counts.

Go race diagnostics cover Go paths, not C/C++ races. No native ASan, allocation/
writer-failure injection, external interposer barrier qualification, power-loss
test, performance measurement or exhaustive concurrency proof is claimed.

## Ambiguities and choices

- No campaign-freeze manifest was supplied; the explicit phase-start instruction
  authorizes maintenance. Candidate tests do not establish gate qualification.
- Bitmap item i maps to bit i, confirmed by mixed/boundary tests; the header does
  not explicitly specify bit order.
- The dispatch cycle restarts at H after process restart. Cycle persistence is
  unspecified; durable priority and cancellation are preserved. Cancelled queued
  entries consume no dispatch turn.
- Evaluate accepts valid high/normal metadata but remains outside persistent
  priority queues and their cycle, sharing the four native slots.
- FIFO and 3:1 apply at mutex-protected selection/slot reservation. Concurrent
  native entries and completions may occur in a different order after dispatch.
- Schema 1 pending records require the specified priority field. Valid predecessor
  data includes normal. Unknown fields remain reserved/ignored; duplicate object
  keys and invalid known fields are rejected.
- Oversized request lines are rejected then disconnected; unterminated EOF
  fragments are discarded. Receipts/indexes remain cached and retained without
  expiry; startup scans all rows, as in the predecessor.

